public import TauCeti.GroupTheory.Presentation.Coxeter

/-!
# A transcribed presentation of the second Fischer group

This file carries the `Fi₂₃` row of the sporadic presentation data required by milestone S1 of
`TauCetiRoadmap/CFSGStatement/README.md`. It records presentation `GPLTable.Fi23.1` from Roderik
Lindenbergh's Group Presentations Library as a `TauCeti.GroupPresentation`, together with the exact
source, generator convention, transcription notes, expected counts, and decidable checks on the
transcription.

The presentation uses the Coxeter graph

```text
a --3-- b --3-- c --3-- d --3-- e --3-- f --3-- g --3-- j
                              |
                              3
                              |
                              h --3-- i
```

where all ten generators are involutions and every omitted edge has label `2`. The source encodes
this graph by the two paths `a3b3c3d3e3f3g3j,d3h3i`. It then appends

```text
(d c b d e f d h i)^10,  (a b c d e f h)^9.
```

Thus the graph contributes ten square relations, nine label-three relations, and thirty-six
omitted-edge relations. With the two appended words, the presentation has `57` relators and its
compiled words have `371` signed letters. The theorems below check those figures and cyclic
reduction from the transcribed data.

The library identifies this record as a presentation of `Fi₂₃` originating in Praeger--Soicher,
whose construction uses low-rank representations and graphs for sporadic groups. As a separate
computational check, GAP 4.15.1 enumerates `31671` cosets of the subgroup generated by `a` through
`i`; the source identifies that subgroup as `2·Fi₂₂`, and `31671` is its index in `Fi₂₃`. This
computation is provenance rather than a Lean theorem: this file asserts no order, finiteness,
simplicity, or identification result.

## Independent source-to-Lean read-through

An independent read-through used the bytes of `gpl.g` whose SHA-256 digest is
`8b90a7a65317a585b5b74d015083bd2fa3db8a969c117a220d00d7e8773103c8`. The exact source record is

```text
presdef := [ "a3b3c3d3e3f3g3j,d3h3i", ,
    "(dcbdefdhi)^(10),(abcdefh)^9",,  ],
```

With `GPL_PCE := true`, `DeCoxList` and its calls to `AddTrivialRelations` and
`AddNonEdgeRelations` expand that record. The theorem below records the same relations regrouped
by kind: entries 1--10 are the squares of `a,b,c,d,e,f,g,h,i,j`; entries 11--19 are
`(ab)³,(bc)³,(cd)³,(de)³,(ef)³,(fg)³,(gj)³,(dh)³,(hi)³`; and entries 20--55 are the exponent-two
pairs

```text
ac,ad,ae,af,ag,ah,ai,aj, bd,be,bf,bg,bh,bi,bj, ce,cf,cg,ch,ci,cj,
df,dg,di,dj, eg,eh,ei,ej, fh,fi,fj, gh,gi, hj,ij.
```

Entries 56 and 57 are `(dcbdefdhi)¹⁰` and `(abcdefh)⁹`. Translating source letters to zero-based
generator indices gives exactly `fi23Presentation_relatorLetters`: there is no change to any word
or exponent and no dropped or duplicated relation, only the presentation-irrelevant regrouping by
kind just described. This closes the row's S1 source-to-Lean read-through. The separate
`FiniteSimpleGroups` comparison does not apply because that development does not cover `Fi₂₃`.

The row publishes an equation for each of its fields: the transcribed relator expressions with
their generator indices written out, and the provenance a manifest row exists to record. Together
with `TauCeti.GroupPresentation.relators_def` and `TauCeti.GroupPresentation.mem_relatorSet_iff`
the first of those determines the compiled words and the relations defining the presented group, so
a consumer reasons about the row without unfolding it.

## The graph itself, and not only its expansion

The graph is recorded directly as the edge list `TauCeti.Sporadic.fi23Edges` and the simply laced
Coxeter matrix it determines. The generated Coxeter relations are compared with the transcribed
relations by compiled-word membership, accounting for their different expressions and ordering.

The row presents the quotient of the Coxeter group of its graph obtained by imposing the two
additional relations from the source.

## Main definitions and results

* `TauCeti.Sporadic.fi23Presentation`: the GPL finite presentation of the Fischer group `Fi₂₃`.
* `TauCeti.Sporadic.fi23Presentation_transcribed` and the equations for the remaining fields: the
  characterization of the row.
* `TauCeti.Sporadic.fi23Presentation_relatorLetters`: the fifty-seven compiled words checked
  against the decoder output in the independent source-to-Lean read-through.
* `TauCeti.Sporadic.fi23Presentation_map_length_relators`,
  `TauCeti.Sporadic.fi23Presentation_totalLength` and
  `TauCeti.Sporadic.fi23Presentation_relatorsCyclicallyReduced`: the three checks on the compiled
  words.
* `TauCeti.Sporadic.fi23Edges` and `TauCeti.Sporadic.fi23CoxeterMatrix`: the graph the source's two
  paths encode, and its Coxeter matrix.
* `TauCeti.Sporadic.fi23Presentation_mem_map_toWord_iff`: the transcribed relations are the ones
  that matrix generates, together with the two appended words.
* `TauCeti.Sporadic.fi23MulEquivPresentedGroupCoxeterAppend`: the row presents the Coxeter group of
  the graph cut down by those two words.

## References

* R. Lindenbergh, *Group Presentations Library*, version 1.0, record `GPLTable.Fi23.1`,
  <https://doris.tudelft.nl/~rlindenbergh/GPL/gpl.g>.
* C. E. Praeger and L. H. Soicher, *Low Rank Representations and Graphs for Sporadic Groups*,
  Cambridge University Press, 1997, Chapter 4, doi:10.1017/CBO9780511526039.005.
* The presentation-row characterization and audit theorem scaffold is adapted from the Janko-row
  formalization in <https://github.com/TauCetiProject/TauCeti/pull/5283>.
-/

public section

